Does "cult movie" mean something else in English and Polish? Over here a film being popular and critically acclaimed does not prevent it being "kultowy". In fact, one of the most celebrated Polish films of all time, "Seksmisja", is the epitome of "kultowy film". We call a movie that when it becomes an iconic part of culture, rematched, remembered and quoted by devoted fans - there being millions of them is perfectly fine.

I'm surprised by how many posters didn't get HAKA. I thought that if you know anything about Maori people, this is it.

@Andrzej

I think cult status is not achieved when a movie has its first run, but long afterwards, when there develops a certain cultishness among its most ardent fans.  In this sense, anything Star Wars might be considered cultish.  And the original trilogy were three of the most financially successful ones ever.

Star Trek is also cultish, but it's not as good an example, because the original series was not as big a hit, but its longevity would support my stance that something can be both popular and cultish at the same time.

@Andrzej T. Typically in the US a cult film has the life cycle of being an underperformer or even a flop at the box office, which then slowly grows a passionate following over the years, with some people viewing multiple times, obsessively quoting perhaps, often developing a fan community even.

But it’s worth considering why some may consider Pulp Fiction a cult film. Perhaps that due to some of its more extreme goings on it will always feel a bit taboo, and have sort of a cult feel for people who discover it for themselves. I don’t know.

